#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054416 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bf25f86-6b27-5eaa-b1a1-1104478b290a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/id #uuid "59d2e907-e788-5a22-93a9-c49ff94df6ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-190" :question/problem "Given $x^2 + y^2 = \\sin{y}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$, assuming that $y = y(x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} + y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin y) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin y) \\\\\n2x + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\cos y\\frac{dy}{dx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\cos y\\frac{dy}{dx} \\\\\n2y\\frac{dy}{dx} - \\cos y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -2x \\\\\n(2y - \\cos y)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -2x \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\frac{2x}{2y - \\cos y} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{2x}{\\cos y - 2y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054417 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2311a895-587e-5609-8a9f-9963b8cae372" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "8a6eb51f-6232-5a08-acf1-136f0d14a4d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-191" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$ for the curve $xy+y^2=2x^2+4$." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y = y(x)$.\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xy + y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{2} + 4) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) + \\frac{d}{dx}(y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{2} + 4) \\\\\nx\\frac{dy}{dx} + y\\frac{d}{dx}(x) + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 4x \\\\\nx\\frac{dy}{dx} + y + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 4x \\\\\n(x + 2y)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 4x - y \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{4x - y}{x + 2y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} m &=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\left(\\dfrac{4x-y}{x+2y}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{4\\cdot 1-2}{1+2\\cdot 2} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{2}{5} \\,. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfdb93d1-d550-5939-8a47-99bd1d863a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Intercept of a Tangent or Normal Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/id #uuid "cd630daa-4813-5ca8-b3d4-5fe35c4af4fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-192" :question/problem "The equation $x^2-2x+y^2+4y=5$ represents a circle. The normal line at the point $\\left(0, -5 \\right)$ on the circle intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P$. What are the coordinates of $P$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/57/Source/Images/7c8c9a815718ae4d5afe44e65592f4b8.png)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(0,-5)$. Remember that the slope of the normal line, $m'$, is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nm' = -\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y = y(x)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2-2x+ y^2+4y) &=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5) \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2)-\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (2x)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (y^2)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (4y)&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5) \\\\[5pt] 2x-2+2y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}+4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=0 \\\\[5pt] (2y+4)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=2-2x \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} &=\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} m &=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\left(\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{2-2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot (-5)+4} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{2}{-6} \\\\[5pt] &=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\,. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\dfrac{1}{m}=3\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the normal at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} y-y_1 &=m'\\left(x-x_1 \\right) \\\\[5pt] y+5 &=3\\left(x-0 \\right) \\\\[5pt] y&=3x-5 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0$, so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} y&=3x-5 \\\\[5pt] 0&=3x-5 \\\\[5pt] x&=\\dfrac{5}{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3},\\,0 \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054485 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "619182bf-107d-5217-a306-ec4f7c1109eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form Using the Product Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "af328f52-53a1-5cc1-a542-6cad8eea4c44" :question/math-academy-id "e-6114" :question/problem "Given $xy+y^2=x^2$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$, assuming that $y = y(x)$.\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xy + y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) + \\frac{d}{dx}(y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) \\\\\nx\\frac{dy}{dx} + y\\frac{d}{dx}(x) + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2x \\\\\nx\\frac{dy}{dx} + y + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx\\frac{dy}{dx} + y + 2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2x \\\\\n(x + 2y)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 2x - y \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{2x - y}{x + 2y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bf25f86-6b27-5eaa-b1a1-1104478b290a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0553ee78-4b34-5424-9cb0-0e4952b4d5d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4x}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255788} {:answer/id #uuid "708a5305-3de4-5ee9-8892-04acd808935e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{3{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255789} {:answer/id #uuid "88fefe02-ac34-55ef-a65d-7d78b71319c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255790} {:answer/id #uuid "e81ea11b-ec00-57c2-8f8a-d8d130e37e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)-3y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255791} {:answer/id #uuid "5f55950b-9b68-5cf1-ac1a-efc4e5c8f188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)-{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "708a5305-3de4-5ee9-8892-04acd808935e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{3{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255789} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e800e94-b8e1-55d3-9ef8-a505e05d0335" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255787}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b9a674f-4b08-56bb-b862-861e9645fdbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-108367" :question/problem "Given that ${y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)-2{x}^{2}=5,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)-2{x}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)-2{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})-4x & =0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(3{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)) & =4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4x}{3{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bf25f86-6b27-5eaa-b1a1-1104478b290a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebb8fbb3-1f11-5c80-b171-9272c163bfd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255795} {:answer/id #uuid "fff84e77-13dd-5fde-910c-744cc1bd1e8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\,xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255796} {:answer/id #uuid "81b2b654-0a3c-531c-baea-c5e3982e9786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255797} {:answer/id #uuid "8feabef2-4865-5554-bb31-dd8d8dbf71e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6x}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255798} {:answer/id #uuid "83b8bcc3-16b6-5cac-ad4e-94f1147f43ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\,xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fff84e77-13dd-5fde-910c-744cc1bd1e8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\,xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255796} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f71e1e2-1df1-5803-808b-e660cff87e4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255794}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa279226-e2fd-558c-b6bc-42bf8cf3bf43" :question/math-academy-id "q-108475" :question/problem "Given that $\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)+3{x}^{2}=11,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)+3{x}^{2} & =11 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)+3{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(11) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{log}⁡(y))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+6x & =0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(-6x)(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\,xy\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bf25f86-6b27-5eaa-b1a1-1104478b290a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d920ea29-434a-569e-ad1a-656a8abcf7f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{y}+2}{1+y{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255802} {:answer/id #uuid "08c44cec-9d0f-5424-ae80-2452b84e19de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y{e}^{y}+1}{2+{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255803} {:answer/id #uuid "8379e541-5679-5efc-ba91-84bca21a5cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{y}-1}{2-{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255804} {:answer/id #uuid "7b39c2d7-809d-530a-b5f6-e9d77244a9c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2-{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255805} {:answer/id #uuid "906e80ff-9e64-58d4-942e-b2c11df10b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{y}+1}{2-{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b39c2d7-809d-530a-b5f6-e9d77244a9c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2-{e}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255805} :answer-field/id #uuid "a631e2a6-38b7-5bf7-ae1f-a7b29525dc2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255801}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "366ec3fb-7d34-59c5-9aae-6ef93106c990" :question/math-academy-id "q-108506" :question/problem "Given that  ${e}^{y}=2y-x,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y-x) \\\\ {e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}(2y)}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}(x)}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2-{e}^{y}) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2-{e}^{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255807 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "619182bf-107d-5217-a306-ec4f7c1109eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108910aa-4b5c-50a8-a9a2-fa326a8f0737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}{16{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255809} {:answer/id #uuid "1de795b1-10f4-5433-a992-db5764c61507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18x-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}{16{y}^{3}+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255810} {:answer/id #uuid "a18de979-7ac2-5b79-853d-fe8c240bfebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255811} {:answer/id #uuid "4e825274-6e2d-5d50-b166-beb3f78e6a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}{{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255812} {:answer/id #uuid "df815fea-87ce-5f9c-b91c-06eeab8ef4e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "108910aa-4b5c-50a8-a9a2-fa326a8f0737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}{16{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255809} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2517cec-86c9-5d85-b58a-77b704b1eb33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255808}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bef78af0-8941-5978-8eb2-ce80e83b63d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-108640" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+4{y}^{4}=6{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n Note that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+4{y}^{4}=6{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+4{y}^{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{y}^{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{3}) \\\\ [{x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)]+16{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =18{x}^{2} \\\\ -{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+16{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =18{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(16{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)) & =18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}{16{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "619182bf-107d-5217-a306-ec4f7c1109eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23f0c5c3-6ae6-5dce-a0c7-510a6d18543d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x-7{x}^{3}{y}^{2}}{7{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255816} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf8c24a-a902-566c-8c58-54bc9d85ad27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{7{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255817} {:answer/id #uuid "01beee6f-9903-595d-915a-d3182d8aa74f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-{x}^{3}{y}^{2}}{7{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255818} {:answer/id #uuid "033cece9-040a-504f-afd8-a264de34280a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x-7{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{7{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255819} {:answer/id #uuid "81e29b73-16c9-5567-bd2f-ea1d5524bcdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "033cece9-040a-504f-afd8-a264de34280a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x-7{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{7{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255819} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aa05ce0-58f8-55ef-80cd-c2ee85989e42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255815}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d1d6994-906a-5550-a2fb-3c16fb49d6c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-108655" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}=12{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2} & =12{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{3}{y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{2}) \\\\ [21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+21{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]+6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24x \\\\ 21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+21{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24x-21{x}^{2}{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+6y) & =24x-21{x}^{2}{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{8x-7{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{7{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2311a895-587e-5609-8a9f-9963b8cae372" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad1daaec-b025-54cc-af65-be869f572f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255823} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa1ea18-4820-5015-ab9d-2bba53e46456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255824} {:answer/id #uuid "fd30544b-8548-53c9-afad-6fcd64142e69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255825} {:answer/id #uuid "64f0d3b6-dbbb-570b-b4fb-c15ce417db89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255826} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c319cd-6cc3-5fac-ac92-66b129d31df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad1daaec-b025-54cc-af65-be869f572f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255823} :answer-field/id #uuid "effbd3bf-202d-501e-aa77-3bfc58e9d459" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255822}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d523cf1-86db-5d18-b1bf-7afa6c18399b" :question/math-academy-id "q-108674" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,2)$ for the curve $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =0 \\\\ y(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,2)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(\\pi /3,\\,2)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)})}_{(\\pi /3,\\,2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2311a895-587e-5609-8a9f-9963b8cae372" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "773dcd82-bc62-5c3d-8ed9-cde7368db04e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255830} {:answer/id #uuid "706779c4-d1f8-543e-84a8-9c835bea1c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255831} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3203eb-6a81-5954-ac08-00742873ade2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255832} {:answer/id #uuid "f55382ce-9a87-5ed6-8258-6e2cb0358ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255833} {:answer/id #uuid "9d337356-ad34-5553-9601-c3ffb9658add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f55382ce-9a87-5ed6-8258-6e2cb0358ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255833} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6465dec-122c-5087-bdfa-b9c22343bb82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255829}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cef3e42d-03e3-55a2-998b-078fdab2d8e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-108679" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(4,\\,4)$ for the curve $\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}+{y}^{1/2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(4,\\,4)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & =-{\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfdb93d1-d550-5939-8a47-99bd1d863a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Intercept of a Tangent or Normal Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc3834a-4af0-57a0-a427-4cd96682d558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255837} {:answer/id #uuid "bda95fc9-5266-52ea-bdfa-d379f2232812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255838} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd9c231-36a2-5828-ba48-d7381e87a88d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255839} {:answer/id #uuid "8800e18a-cd98-5083-8673-f6a5607cfb51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255840} {:answer/id #uuid "e50f2a66-98b4-5180-b16e-e5cfffed03ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bda95fc9-5266-52ea-bdfa-d379f2232812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255838} :answer-field/id #uuid "a76785b5-d035-5e65-b1b5-b9b62f5d6b9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255836}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d989707-e79c-566c-8ee3-27f263bf177c" :question/math-academy-id "q-15130" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is ${x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=6.$ The normal line at the point $(-3,\\,1)$ on the curve intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(-3,\\,1).$  Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-2x}{-6y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(-3,\\,1)$ is \n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(-3,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{x}{3y})}_{(-3,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is ${m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-1}=1.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(-3,\\,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-1) & =1(x+3) \\\\ y & =(x+3)+1 \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+4 \\\\ 0 & =x+4 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(-4,\\,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bf25f86-6b27-5eaa-b1a1-1104478b290a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3767cc6f-31bc-5571-83eb-9834781b141c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{1-{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255844} {:answer/id #uuid "220b9d52-1b32-52be-872e-3dd9736708fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255845} {:answer/id #uuid "17628d73-98f4-5909-9be4-bc45cfd50f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255846} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5bdcd6-8a4c-507a-b422-85e80251959a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{y}}{6x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255847} {:answer/id #uuid "4028f038-b715-507c-ba6d-353a19b38de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17628d73-98f4-5909-9be4-bc45cfd50f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255846} :answer-field/id #uuid "16feb5c7-cad2-5193-9e12-09eeb8b60638" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255843}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ba90f706-83ce-5736-9e26-08fdd3389f2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-2289" :question/problem "Given that  $y-{2}^{y}=3{x}^{2},$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}y-{2}^{y} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-{2}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({2}^{y}) & =6x \\\\ (1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2){2}^{y})(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =6x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}) & =6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6x}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{y}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bf25f86-6b27-5eaa-b1a1-1104478b290a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bd8fba3-a903-566d-bf59-729f7b7d6d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(3x)}{y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255851} {:answer/id #uuid "d407e01e-6222-578b-9e62-de7cdb3f20ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{sec}^{2}⁡(3x)}{2y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255852} {:answer/id #uuid "33890605-e540-54b0-9ad0-fdb5fc10f532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255853} {:answer/id #uuid "713ee965-7ec0-524f-9b6c-ce09bdf6c151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{3y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255854} {:answer/id #uuid "7d63c64f-4384-54c3-85ff-160f261a17c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{cot}^{2}⁡(3x)}{2y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d407e01e-6222-578b-9e62-de7cdb3f20ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{sec}^{2}⁡(3x)}{2y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255852} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8cbd7d0-14ef-5262-a1b4-ed66f9ca2ac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255850}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a4e18ef-12cd-5ee3-9a6d-cf3778b4e1b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-2290" :question/problem "Given that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)-{y}^{2}=y,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)-{y}^{2} & =y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)-{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\ 3{sec}^{2}⁡(3x)-2y(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3{sec}^{2}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2y+1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{sec}^{2}⁡(3x)}{2y+1}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2311a895-587e-5609-8a9f-9963b8cae372" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5e87ee5-0c4a-5cfb-8b64-a340535c826d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5e87ee5-0c4a-5cfb-8b64-a340535c826d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255858} :answer-field/id #uuid "b95b92a0-3109-5324-bf41-0b1c873f9cef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255857}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50d30d9c-6ebb-56fa-a396-2394eb5e50c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-341915" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(16,\\,4)$ for the curve $\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}=6.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}+{y}^{1/2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(16,\\,4)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(16,\\,4)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}})|}_{(16,\\,4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfdb93d1-d550-5939-8a47-99bd1d863a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Intercept of a Tangent or Normal Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a754b56-bd84-527a-9eb5-627286d3f26a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a754b56-bd84-527a-9eb5-627286d3f26a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255861} :answer-field/id #uuid "56c1c764-e810-5280-b9f5-688edb7a227f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255860} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "657ae0bc-0cb8-5764-ac20-e992138af162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "657ae0bc-0cb8-5764-ac20-e992138af162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255863} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b3b1473-851b-54e1-be56-fe2b4a16efe2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255862}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2bec85f4-6642-5907-9fd0-41128942f8dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-342321" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is ${x}^{2}+xy=6{y}^{2}.$ The normal line at the point $(2,1)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(2,1).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy & =6{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) \\\\ 2x+(y+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ 2x+y+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x-y \\\\ (x-12y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-2x-y}{x-12y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{-2x-y}{x-12y})|}_{(2,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(2)-1}{2-12(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-5)}{(-10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the normal at the point $(2,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-2(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =-2x+4 \\\\ y & =-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+5 \\\\ y & =-2(0)+5 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfdb93d1-d550-5939-8a47-99bd1d863a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Intercept of a Tangent or Normal Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92e4d264-8bdc-5977-8c28-9c38699fa51e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92e4d264-8bdc-5977-8c28-9c38699fa51e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255866} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a015aab-f223-537b-93ff-1f1a4ea397e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255865} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6561f3e-6cd1-5080-b35b-e296de6c487e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6561f3e-6cd1-5080-b35b-e296de6c487e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255868} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf228e6f-299e-5240-b089-37d5e76253c1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255867}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bfaeba2-34c5-5317-adf8-a3f1eb304fa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-342325" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$ The tangent line at the point $(0,2)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(0,2).$ \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(36) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(36) \\\\ 8x+18y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 18y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{8x}{18y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{4x}{9y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(0,2)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,\\,2)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{4x}{9y})|}_{(0,\\,2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\cdot 0}{9\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent line at the point $(0,2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =0(x-0) \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n(0,\\,2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfdb93d1-d550-5939-8a47-99bd1d863a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Intercept of a Tangent or Normal Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce7de615-96e6-5fbd-93db-49ed1c85552a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255871} {:answer/id #uuid "1eff946d-93db-5ddb-aae8-ab95cb1ca3a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eff946d-93db-5ddb-aae8-ab95cb1ca3a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255872} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e8ac9fd-9daf-5450-be75-eeb47061e84e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255870} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28020db3-8c15-50b9-bbfd-acdbaafad98f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28020db3-8c15-50b9-bbfd-acdbaafad98f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255874} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d86ba02-0342-5ec0-91f0-d1af3a6bf947" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255873}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6bf2822c-2fe8-5e06-b637-67fd4c8ecb6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342387" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is ${x}^{2}+2y=20.$ The tangent line at the point $(2,8)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(2,8).$ \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2y & =20 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20) \\\\ 2x+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,8)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,\\,8)} \\\\  & ={(-x)|}_{(2,\\,8)} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent line at the point $(2,8)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-8 & =-2(x-2) \\\\ y-8 & =-2x+4 \\\\ y & =-2x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-2x+12 \\\\ 2x & =12 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(6,\\,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2311a895-587e-5609-8a9f-9963b8cae372" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7aa27835-773a-507b-b9a4-b037a51179df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255877} {:answer/id #uuid "6544c4ee-6b5a-5b70-98fa-27063b586f84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255878} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc56c95-4ba5-5d0f-9ff9-e937f52c3f79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255879} {:answer/id #uuid "a226a6d2-1362-5548-9c31-88ddaa628f87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255880} {:answer/id #uuid "33c35d4b-b8a6-58fb-946b-60fc3841edc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aa27835-773a-507b-b9a4-b037a51179df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255877} :answer-field/id #uuid "13be0153-e06b-582a-8134-73dab919400c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255876}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c55237e-c942-5772-aab9-53dcc871dac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-53745" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,-1)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}y=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-1) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,-1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,-1)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2y}{x})}_{(1,\\,-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2311a895-587e-5609-8a9f-9963b8cae372" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb39b8d2-a7f1-533a-b4f7-2be722af37dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053323} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f8240d-c020-544d-bf78-3d186e783af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053324} {:answer/id #uuid "331307e8-2170-55f8-936d-6b74170340c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053325} {:answer/id #uuid "7301ee39-e742-5f38-bbc5-fbb71d864d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053326} {:answer/id #uuid "7a641cad-eaa0-57c7-9f10-70981e15c329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a641cad-eaa0-57c7-9f10-70981e15c329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053327} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dbca48e-b149-5a4e-9fe0-d8e499c7c9d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053322}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "421782a5-f8ee-574b-bd35-7a68ca94b6ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-53746" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(0, 1)$ for the curve $x^{2} + xy - y^{2} =-1$." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y-2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ (2x+y)+(x-2y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x+y}{x-2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2x+y}{x-2y})}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 0+1}{0-2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{0+1}{0-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfdb93d1-d550-5939-8a47-99bd1d863a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Intercept of a Tangent or Normal Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aef06b2-7005-55ee-b236-587964e2d753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255884} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6c25d9-e6a8-5177-a8ef-52a0e44e7424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255885} {:answer/id #uuid "33b93caf-b4ed-55a2-ad0f-2856277a617a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255886} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f6ea62-9cac-500f-b3ba-24923a5dbed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255887} {:answer/id #uuid "c576687f-4099-56aa-b62a-4a59e5d3cbd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c576687f-4099-56aa-b62a-4a59e5d3cbd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255888} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eaac560-d733-5230-9f9d-912c943df198" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255883}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a005b573-01a6-5ae1-85c6-8a86b21af397" :question/math-academy-id "q-53750" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13947863/assets/faa8a8e0521972fa860d474ac079405b6930df6c47fda05808fdf242d6d6922b.png)\n\nThe equation $xy=5$ represents a hyperbola. The tangent line at the point $(1,5)$ on the hyperbola intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,5).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,5)$  is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,5)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{y}{x})}_{(1,\\,5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-5) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ y & =-5(x-1)+5 \\\\ y & =-5x+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+10 \\\\ y & =-5\\cdot 0+10 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,10).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "619182bf-107d-5217-a306-ec4f7c1109eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "487fbd07-9374-5d31-8d4f-9c4b79c04a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6-2xy}{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255891} {:answer/id #uuid "fc4c43c9-66b3-5c14-b539-20164e1a9b11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x+2xy}{{x}^{2}-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255892} {:answer/id #uuid "ee35deda-25a0-5f19-80ba-68e806e1b457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6x-xy}{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255893} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3a113c-fbcb-59eb-bf43-f6588bdd998c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}-2xy}{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255894} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab38bcc-674c-56e3-a491-13f4e44434c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x-2y}{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb3a113c-fbcb-59eb-bf43-f6588bdd998c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}-2xy}{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255894} :answer-field/id #uuid "c55b6534-9500-51da-a551-b8c385830489" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255890}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef2b8cc4-5338-5352-affe-2a503c21ffcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-72260" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}y+4y=2{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+4y & =2{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y+4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ [{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x]+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+4) & =6{x}^{2}-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}-2xy}{{x}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "619182bf-107d-5217-a306-ec4f7c1109eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f0ebcb1-c9b1-578e-a637-7963fb5ce363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2y}{x+12{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255898} {:answer/id #uuid "0061dc96-072a-57c0-b764-d641af5bc448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-y}{x+6{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255899} {:answer/id #uuid "7616b0c2-2be7-52ff-8af5-ffea197e63e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+y}{3x-6{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255900} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a2d671-915c-55fa-b29a-2d85c2c3b753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-y}{2x+12{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255901} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7daaf2-2a39-530d-95f9-77db4a676275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-y}{x+3{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0061dc96-072a-57c0-b764-d641af5bc448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-y}{x+6{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255899} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fabf7c7-db80-573b-80c0-2e7ce575fefe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255897}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ea99235-25dc-50d1-8b6b-8640c1a16529" :question/math-academy-id "q-72262" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $2xy+4{y}^{3}={x}^{2}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $2xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy+4{y}^{3} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2xy+4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-1) \\\\ [2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y]+12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y+12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x-2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2x+12{y}^{2}) & =2x-2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-2y}{2x+12{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{x+6{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "619182bf-107d-5217-a306-ec4f7c1109eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e65ebdc4-2a86-584f-85c1-4995985a95d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x-3xy}{2{x}^{3}+4y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255905} {:answer/id #uuid "bba7eb30-7209-5123-84be-2422af603ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x-6{x}^{2}y}{2{x}^{3}+4y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255906} {:answer/id #uuid "7af4bfe9-0967-5e82-ac64-b159e7aa8b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x-6xy}{2{x}^{2}+4y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255907} {:answer/id #uuid "43bfd979-89de-51e7-ac92-56495cbc5e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x+6xy}{2{x}^{3}-4y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255908} {:answer/id #uuid "200d7963-fe78-54ad-95a2-f1545164af7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-6xy}{2{x}^{3}+4y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bba7eb30-7209-5123-84be-2422af603ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x-6{x}^{2}y}{2{x}^{3}+4y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255906} :answer-field/id #uuid "42102d9d-5ac6-5acb-81f2-aecc531eeeb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255904}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4275802-53c8-5e1e-8981-df33206ad101" :question/math-academy-id "q-72263" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $2{x}^{3}y+2{y}^{2}=4{x}^{2}+6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $2{x}^{3}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}y+2{y}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}+6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}y+2{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+6) \\\\ [2{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 6{x}^{2}]+4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8x \\\\ 2{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6{x}^{2}y+4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8x-6{x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}+4y) & =8x-6{x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{8x-6{x}^{2}y}{2{x}^{3}+4y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
